\section{Shared Generation Is Not an Independent Oracle}

A generated assembler, simulator, and processor may all agree for the wrong reason. If a description places an immediate in the wrong bits, an assembler and a decoder generated from it might agree on incorrect bytes. Likewise, if an ISA model gets a sign extension wrong, two backends that consume the same model can reproduce the error. Agreement is useful for finding differences between backends, but it does not settle whether the common source is correct. A test plan needs an external check for each shared assumption that matters.

Sections~4.6 and~7 of the VADL paper describe trace generation and co-simulation, and Section~5 gives separate evaluations for the generated components \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. Adopt that separation in the test harness. Encoding tests should compare bytes against independently specified fields; execution tests can feed an independent binary to both the generated machine and a reference model. Compiler tests should start with source programs of defined behavior and compare their results, not insist that two compilers select the same instructions. \Cref{fig:visual-337} shows where an independent byte check sits relative to the execution check.

