\section{Make Small Fixtures That Check More Than Round Trips}

The following VADL macro invocation is from the RV32I appendix, Listing~29, with spacing normalized:

\begin{verbatim}
$ItypeInstr(ADDI; +; 0b000; SInt)
\end{verbatim}

The model behind the invocation groups instruction behavior, encoding, and assembly syntax. That makes it easy to keep them synchronized, but also makes them a poor source of independent expected values. For \texttt{addi x5, x0, -1}, take the ADDI format from the \href{https://docs.riscv.org/reference/isa/unpriv/rv32.html}{RISC-V ISA manual}. Placing the 12-bit immediate \texttt{0xfff} at bits 31--20, source index 0 at bits 19--15, function bits 0 at bits 14--12, destination index 5 at bits 11--7, and opcode \texttt{0x13} at bits 6--0 gives word \texttt{0xfff00293}. In a little-endian image the bytes are \texttt{93 02 f0 ff}. Testing the disassembler may compare decoded operands or normalized text rather than demanding the same alias spelling in every tool. Include the immediate endpoints \(-2048\) and \(2047\): testing only small positive values can miss both an incorrect signed range and a bad sign extension.

A linker fixture should likewise check the effect, not just that the generated executable loads. A small table that refers to a symbol in a second object can be inspected before linking for the expected relocation and after linking for the resolved address. Repeat with the symbol moved to catch a missing or double-applied adjustment. An ABI fixture can call a separately compiled function with distinct argument values and verify a callee-saved register afterward. Simply calling generated code from generated code risks letting the same calling-convention mistake pass at both ends.

