\section{Find the First Architectural Disagreement}

Commit comparison is useful for a deterministic, single-core test with matched initial state and scripted external inputs. Ignore extra pipeline cycles at this boundary, but keep architectural effects. The pseudocode is illustrative, not a VADL test language.

\begin{verbatim}
ref = reference(load_fixture(), initial_state)
dut = generated_core(load_fixture(), initial_state)
for i in range(expected_events):
    a = ref.next_event_within_limit()
    b = dut.next_event_within_limit()
    require equal(pc, outcome, register_delta, stores, a, b)
require ref.done() and dut.done()     # no missing or extra effects
\end{verbatim}

Here an event is a committed instruction or a synchronous trap, and \texttt{outcome} includes the next PC and any trap cause. Its store record should include address, byte enables, and data; any architecturally visible control or status update belongs in the event too. Start at \texttt{0x100} with mutable registers zeroed and run the ADDI fixture, then \texttt{addi x0, x0, 9}, then \texttt{addi x6, x0, 0}. The first event writes all ones to \(x_5\); a missing sign extension instead yields \(4095\). The second must not change \(x_0\), and the third must write zero to \(x_6\). The invariant is equality of observable event prefixes. A separate cycle-aligned comparison between the CAS and HDL can then find, for example, an early write that did not change that sequence.

\section{Report Coverage and Its Limits}

Section~5.5 of the paper reports use of compliance cases, handwritten assembly, and small C programs with the generated tools. Section~5.8 reports Embench and compliance validation of the simulated generated hardware. Neither covers all legal states, all devices, or every permitted memory behavior. Section~5.4 is narrower still for the compiler: four of 22 Embench programs completed successfully with the generated setup, and comparison was at \texttt{-O0}. Label that as end-to-end evidence for those programs, not general compiler validation. Preserve the failing input, versions, configuration, and first divergent event when a test does fail; the smallest reliable mismatch is often more valuable than another successful benchmark.
